.TH cal "" "Command"
.SH NAME
cal \- print a calendar
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBcal [\fImonth\fB] [\fIyear\fB]\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
cal -- print a calendar
Usage:	cal [ month ] [ year ]
.HE
.B cal
prints a calendar.
With no arguments, it prints a calendar for the current month.
.PP
With one argument: if the argument is numeric it is taken as a
.I year
and
.B cal
prints a twelve-month calendar for that year; otherwise the argument is
taken as a
.I month
and
.B cal
prints that month of the current year.
.PP
With two arguments, one gives the month and the other the year, in
either order, provided the month is not itself given as a plain
number (in which case the first argument is the month and the
second is the year).
.PP
A
.I month
may be given as a decimal number from 1 to 12, as an upper- or
lower-case English or French name (in full, or abbreviated to three
letters, with or without a trailing period), or as an upper- or
lower-case roman numeral from
.B i
to
.BR xii .
A
.I year
must be in the range 1 through 9999.
.PP
.B cal
accounts for the Gregorian calendar reform: England and its colonies
skipped 11 days after 3 September 1752, so weekday calculations differ
before and after that date.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.B date
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.B cal
prints a usage message and exits with nonzero status if it is given
more than two arguments, or if the resulting month or year is out of
range.
